    "case_id": "mxh075",
    "case_summary": "A male neonate, aged 2 weeks, is brought to primary care with symptoms of excessive sleepiness, feeding difficulties, and grunting. The infant was delivered at home at approximately 38 weeks gestation without prenatal care. Initially healthy, the infant's condition has gradually deteriorated since birth. The mother, aged 30, reports no significant medical history, hereditary conditions, or drug use during pregnancy. Current vital signs show: temperature 98.6 ºF, blood pressure 80/40 mmHg, heart rate 200/min, and respiratory rate 70/min. Physical examination reveals nasal flaring, costosternal retractions, and perioral cyanosis. Cardiac auscultation demonstrates a single S2 and a systolic ejection murmur. The liver is enlarged on abdominal palpation, and all peripheral pulses are bounding. Chest imaging shows an enlarged cardiac silhouette with increased pulmonary vasculature. What is the most likely underlying pathology in this case?\n\nOptions:\nA. Tetralogy of Fallot\nB. Patent ductus arteriosus\nC. Double outlet right ventricle\nD. Hypoplastic left heart syndrome\nE. Ebstein anomaly\nF. Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection\nG. Tricuspid atresia\nH. Transposition of the great arteries\nI. Atrioventricular septal defect\nJ. Truncus arteriosus\n",
